ecial  moment that just it just drew me through  it  um and then the realization like wow  look where i've landed  you know i think no matter what we  achieve in life it's important to look  at where we are like where do we think  we'd be and where are we now and what  are we proud of right now  um in every class and every conversation  with a student i always ask what are you  proud of because i think we forget to  ask ourselves that  so um yeah i think the moments where  i've been able to really sort of sit  back and be like whoa this is a very  cool thing that music enables those are  probably my favorite performances  thank you  [Laughter]  oh that's so  cool i'll let you select this uh  maybe the person in the jean jacket  that's you yeah  that is that is you  red shirt and jean jacket yes  do you need a mic or a person  you can say it and they can translate it  yeah  okay  living dangerously okay  thank you so much for coming to yale and  giving your talk  it was really wonderful to hear you play  i'm a master student at the school of  music  one of the things that i'm very  passionate about is the intersection of  media and  music and one of your recent projects  was with david lang and involved ai and  i was curious about how that came about  and how you see the future of music  interacting with  the  new forms of media that is currently  emerging and always changing and  and transforming our art form  that was part of a  project that i co-founded so that was  something that i was working on a lot  during  my  part of my sabbatical and also part of  the season that mostly was canceled last  season  there's an ai scientist named carol  o'reilly  who um  she she and i go way back socially and  we have been talking on the phone about  just a brainstorm of like where does  music and ai  overlap and i love talking with people  from different fields and kind of  interpreting their knowledge into a  musical or artistic  spectrum kind of like when i go to a  museum 